# Put an urgent job first with Lateralus queue

A worker has two jobs waiting: resize a photo and update the search index. An
alert comes in and needs to go first. With `stdlib/queue.ltl`, you can put the
alert at the front and keep the regular jobs in arrival order.

```lateralus
let jobs = new()
jobs = push_back(jobs, "resize-photo")
jobs = push_back(jobs, "update-index")
jobs = push_front(jobs, "send-alert")
```

Each push returns a new queue. Save that result before the next operation.
`pop_front(jobs)` returns a pair: the job at index 0 and the remaining queue at
index 1. Update `jobs` with the second value so the next pop advances the queue.

```lateralus
while !is_empty(jobs) {
    let result = pop_front(jobs)
    println(result[0])
    jobs = result[1]
}
```

## Run the example

From the repository root, join the module and example into one source file.
The Python runner needs both in the same compilation unit for this module.
The example uses the exported functions from the checked-out module.

```sh
python -c "from pathlib import Path; Path('queue_tutorial.ltl').write_text(Path('stdlib/queue.ltl').read_text(encoding='utf-8') + chr(10) + Path('examples/queue_tutorial.ltl').read_text(encoding='utf-8'), encoding='utf-8')"
python -m lateralus_lang run queue_tutorial.ltl
```

The example checks the queue after each pop before printing the jobs:

```text
send-alert
resize-photo
update-index
```

The empty-queue check expects `[nil, []]`. The loop uses `is_empty` to stop
before trying to process that value.
